= Pulse =

== Overview ==
A '''pulse''' is the rhythmic pressure wave associated with heartbeats and is commonly used to measure heart rate as beats per minute.

== Definition ==
In routine clinical use, pulse or heart rate is the number of heartbeats in a period of time, usually one minute. A pulse can be felt where an artery passes near the skin.

== Scope & importance ==
Pulse measurement is a basic vital-sign observation. Rate, rhythm, and context can provide information about cardiovascular function and physiological state.

== Core concepts ==
Common pulse sites include the wrist and neck. Heart rate varies with age, activity, body position, medications, temperature, fitness, stress, and health conditions.

== Examples ==
A clinician may count the radial pulse at the wrist. Wearable sensors can estimate pulse rate optically, while an electrocardiogram measures cardiac electrical activity by a different method.

== Applications / implications ==
Pulse is used in routine examinations, exercise monitoring, emergency assessment, inpatient care, and many physiological measurements.

== Epistemic notes ==
A pulse value is context-dependent and is not a diagnosis by itself. Measurement method, rhythm irregularity, activity state, symptoms, and individual factors affect interpretation.

== Clinical interpretation and measurement ==
The '''pulse rate''' is the number of palpable arterial pulsations per minute; '''rhythm''' describes whether successive pulsations occur at regular intervals. These are related but distinct observations. A manual pulse count may differ from an electrical heart-rate measurement when not every cardiac beat produces an effective palpable arterial pulse. A sensor or wearable estimate should not be assumed to reproduce a clinician's manual examination in every rhythm.

Measurements need context: recent activity, posture, temperature, medications, pain, stress and the person's clinical circumstances can affect the rate. Counting for a full minute is useful when the rhythm is irregular or precision matters. Rate alone cannot identify the cause of an abnormal finding.

== Connection to blood pressure ==
[[Related to::Blood pressure|Blood pressure]] measures arterial pressure, whereas the pulse is a wave produced by cardiac ejection and detected at an arterial site. A normal palpable pulse does not by itself establish normal blood pressure, and one measurement does not substitute for the other. Both are observations interpreted alongside other vital signs and symptoms.

== Example and limits ==
A person's pulse may increase during exertion and later fall during recovery; interpreting that change requires the measurement conditions and individual baseline. A single value is not proof of cardiovascular disease or health.

